== Affections Raised Very High

#align(center)[Showing what are no certain Signs that religious Affections are truly gracious, or that they are not.]

#smallcaps[If] anyone, on reading what has just been said, is ready to acquit himself and say, “I am not one of those who have no religious affections; I am often greatly moved when I consider the great things of religion,” let him not be content with this, that he has religious affections. For, as was observed before, we ought not to reject and condemn all affections, as though true religion did not consist in affection at all. But on the other hand, we ought not to approve of all, as though everyone who was religiously affected had true grace and was in that the subject of the saving influences of the Spirit of God. The right way, therefore, is to distinguish among religious affections, between one sort and another. So let us now try to do this, and to that end I would do two things.

I. I would mention some things which are no signs one way or the other, either that affections are such as true religion consists in, or that they are otherwise, so that we may be guarded against judging affections by false signs.

II. I would observe some things in which those affections that are spiritual and gracious differ from those that are not, and by which they may be distinguished and known.

FIRST, I would take notice of some things which are no signs that affections are gracious, or that they are not.

I. It is no sign one way or the other that religious affections are very great, or raised very high.

Some are ready to condemn all high affections. If persons appear to have their religious affections raised to an extraordinary pitch, they are prejudiced against them and decide that they are delusions, without further inquiry. But if it is as has been proved, that true religion lies very much in religious affections, then it follows that if there is a great deal of true religion, there will be great religious affections. If true religion in the hearts of men is raised to a great height, divine and holy affections will be raised to a great height.

Love is an affection. But will any Christian say that men ought not to love God and Jesus Christ in a high degree? And will anyone say that we ought not to have a very great hatred of sin and a very deep sorrow for it? Or that we ought not to exercise a high degree of gratitude to God for the mercies we receive from him, and for the great things he has done for the salvation of fallen men? Or that we should not have very great and strong desires after God and holiness? Is there anyone who will profess that his affections in religion are great enough, and will say, “I have no cause to be humbled that I am no more affected with the things of religion than I am; I have no reason to be ashamed that I have no greater exercises of love to God, and sorrow for sin, and gratitude for the mercies I have received”? Who is there who will go and bless God that he is affected enough with what he has read and heard of the wonderful love of God to worms and rebels in giving his only begotten Son to die for them, and of the dying love of Christ? And who will pray that he may not be affected with them in any higher degree, because high affections are improper and very unlovely in Christians, being enthusiastic and ruinous to true religion?

Our text plainly speaks of great and high affections when it speaks of #emph[rejoicing with joy unspeakable and full of glory.] Here the most superlative expressions the language can afford are used. And the Scriptures often require us to exercise very high affections. So in the first and great commandment of the law there is a heaping up of expressions, as though words were lacking to express the degree to which we ought to love God: “Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind and with all your strength.” So the saints are called on to exercise high degrees of joy. “Rejoice,” says Christ to his disciples, “and be exceedingly glad” (Matt 5:12). So it is said in Ps 68:3, “Let the righteous be glad; let them rejoice before God; let them rejoice exceedingly.” So in the same book of Psalms the saints are often called on #emph[to shout for joy,] and in Luke 6:23 #emph[to leap for joy.] So they are abundantly called on to exercise high degrees of gratitude for mercies: to #emph[praise God with all their hearts, with hearts lifted up in the ways of the Lord, and their souls magnifying the Lord, singing his praises, talking of his wondrous works, declaring his deeds,] and so on.

And we find the most eminent saints in Scripture often professing high affections. So the psalmist #emph[speaks] of his love as if it were #emph[unspeakable:] “Oh, how I love Your law!” (Ps 119:97). So he expresses a great degree of hatred of sin: “Do I not hate those who hate You, O LORD, and detest those who rise against You? I hate them with perfect hatred” (Ps 139:21–22). He also expresses a high degree of sorrow for sin. He speaks of his sins #emph[going over his head, as a heavy burden that was too heavy for him, and of his roaring all day long,] and #emph[his moisture being turned into the drought of summer,] and #emph[his bones being] as it were #emph[broken] with sorrow. So he often expresses great degrees of spiritual desire, in a multitude of the strongest expressions that can be conceived, such as #emph[his longing, his soul thirsting as a dry and weary land where there is no water, his panting, his flesh and heart crying out, his soul breaking with the longing it has,] and so on. He expresses the exercise of great and extreme grief for the sins of others: “My eyes shed streams of tears because Your law is not obeyed” (Ps 119:136). And in verse 53: “Horror has taken hold of me because of the wicked who reject Your law.” He expresses high exercises of joy: “O LORD, the king rejoices in Your strength. How greatly he exults in Your salvation!” (Ps 21:1). “When I sing praise to You my lips will shout for joy” (Ps 71:23). “Because Your loving devotion is better than life, my lips will glorify You. So I will bless You as long as I live; in Your name I will lift my hands. My soul is satisfied as with the richest of foods; with joyful lips my mouth will praise You. When I remember You on my bed, I think of You through the watches of the night. For You are my help; I will sing for joy in the shadow of Your wings” (Ps 63:3–7).

The apostle #emph[Paul] expresses high exercises of affection. So he expresses the exercise of pity and concern for the good of others, even to #emph[anguish of heart; a great, fervent] and #emph[abundant love,] and #emph[earnest] and #emph[longing desires,] and #emph[exceeding joy.] He speaks of the #emph[exultation] and #emph[triumphs] of his soul, and his #emph[earnest expectation] and #emph[hope,] and his #emph[abundant tears,] and the #emph[travail of his soul,] in pity, grief, earnest desires, godly jealousy and fervent zeal, in many places that have been cited already, which I therefore need not repeat. #emph[John the Baptist] expressed #emph[great joy] (John 3:29). Those blessed #emph[women] who anointed the body of Jesus are represented as in a very high exercise of religious affection on the occasion of Christ’s resurrection: “So they hurried away from the tomb in fear and great joy” (Matt 28:8).

It is often foretold of the church of God, in her future happy seasons here on earth, that they will rejoice exceedingly: “They will walk, O LORD, in the light of Your presence. They will rejoice in Your name all day long, and in Your righteousness they will exult” (Ps 89:15–16). “Rejoice greatly, O Daughter of Zion! Shout in triumph, O Daughter of Jerusalem! See, your King comes to you,” and so on (Zech 9:9). The same is represented in countless other places. And because high degrees of joy are the proper and genuine fruits of the gospel of Christ, the angel calls this gospel #emph[good news of great joy that will be for all the people.]

The saints and angels in heaven, who have religion in its highest perfection, are exceedingly affected with what they see and contemplate of God’s perfections and works. They are all like a pure heavenly flame of fire in their love, and in the greatness and strength of their joy and gratitude. Their praises are represented #emph[as the sound of many waters, and as the sound of a great thunder.] Now the only reason why their affections are so much higher than the holy affections of saints on earth is that they see the things they are affected by more according to their truth, and have their affections more conformed to the nature of things. And therefore, if religious affections in men here below are of the same nature and kind as theirs, the higher they are, and the nearer they come to theirs in degree, the better. For in that they will be so much the more conformed to truth, as theirs are.

From these things it certainly appears that religious affections being in a very high degree is no evidence that they are not such as have the nature of true religion. Therefore those greatly err who condemn persons as enthusiasts merely because their affections are very high.

And on the other hand, it is no evidence that religious affections are of a spiritual and gracious nature because they are great. It is very plain from the holy Scripture, our sure and infallible rule for judging things of this nature, that there are religious affections which are very high and yet are not spiritual and saving. The apostle #emph[Paul] speaks of affections in the #emph[Galatians] which had been exceedingly elevated, and yet he plainly speaks of them as fearing that they were vain and had come to nothing: “What then has become of your blessing? For I can testify that, if it were possible, you#super[+] would have torn out your#super[+] eyes and given them to me” (Gal 4:15). And in the eleventh verse he tells them #emph[he was afraid for them, that he had spent his labor on them in vain.] So the children of #emph[Israel] were greatly affected with God’s mercy to them when they had seen how wonderfully he worked for them at the Red Sea, where they #emph[sang God’s praise,] though they soon forgot his works. So they were greatly affected again at Mount #emph[Sinai,] when they saw the marvelous manifestations God made of himself there. They seemed mightily engaged in their minds, and with great eagerness they answered, when God proposed his holy covenant to them, “All that the LORD has spoken we will do, and we will be obedient.” But how soon there was an end to all this mighty eagerness and engagement of affection! How quickly they turned aside after other gods, rejoicing and shouting around their golden calf! So great multitudes who were affected with the miracle of raising #emph[Lazarus] from the dead were elevated to a high degree and made a mighty stir when Jesus, soon after, entered #emph[Jerusalem.] They exceedingly magnified Christ, as though the ground were not good enough for the donkey he rode to tread on. So they cut down branches of palm trees and spread them in the way; indeed, they pulled off their garments and spread them in the way. And they cried with loud voices, “Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!” so as to make the whole city ring again and put everyone in an uproar. We learn from the evangelist #emph[John] that the reason the people made this stir was that they were affected with the miracle of raising #emph[Lazarus] (John 12:18). Here was a vast multitude crying #emph[Hosanna] on this occasion, so that it gave the Pharisees occasion to say, “Look how the whole world has gone after Him!” (John 12:19). But Christ had at that time only a few true disciples. And how quickly this stir came to an end! All of this kind is quelled and dead when this #emph[Jesus] stands bound, with a mock robe and a crown of thorns, to be mocked, spat on, scourged, condemned and executed. Indeed, there was a great and loud outcry about him among the multitude then, as well as before, but of a very different kind. It is not then #emph[Hosanna, Hosanna,] but #emph[crucify, crucify.]

And it is the united voice of all orthodox divines that there may be religious affections raised to a very high degree, and yet nothing of true religion be there. #footnote[#emph[Mr.] Stoddard #emph[observes that common affections are sometimes stronger than saving ones.] Guide to Christ. P. 21.]
